<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicolae Ceaușescu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nicolae Ceaușescu was the communist leader of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> from 1965 to 1989. He won Western praise for keeping distance from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, but his rule became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>'s harshest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorships</a>, with a pervasive secret police, a personality cult and severe austerity to repay foreign debt. He was overthrown in the revolution of December 1989 and executed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicolae-ceausescu.jpg" alt="Nicolae Ceaușescu" width="330" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Attribution · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolae_Ceaușescu_(3x4_cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ceaușescu was born in the village of Scornicești, the third of nine children of a poor peasant family. His father owned about 3 hectares of land and a few sheep, and Nicolae helped the family income by tailoring. He left the village school at eleven for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>. He joined the Romanian Communist Party in his teens and was repeatedly imprisoned under the pre-war and wartime regimes for his activism. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> he rose through the party under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gheorghe-gheorghiu-dej">Gheorghe Gheorghiu-Dej</a>. As a high official in the agricultural and defence ministries he had a large part in forced collectivisation, during which over 80,000 peasants were arrested between 1949 and 1952, according to the party's own data.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Gheorghiu-Dej died on 19 March 1965, Ceaușescu was not the obvious successor, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a> chose him as a compromise candidate and elected him general secretary on 22 March. He eased press censorship, and in a speech on 21 August 1968 he condemned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact-invasion-of-czechoslovakia">Warsaw Pact invasion of Czechoslovakia</a>, which raised his popularity. In October 1966 he had issued Decree 770 restricting abortion and contraception, which led to child abandonment and swelled the orphanages. After visiting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-korea">North Korea</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and North Vietnam in 1971 he began to emulate North Korea's system. He was head of state from 1967 and in 1974 made the presidency an executive office. The Securitate carried out mass surveillance and repression.</p><p>Western governments briefly saw him as an anti-Soviet maverick and lent to him, and he borrowed more than 13 billion dollars from the West. Plans to make <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> a major oil refiner failed, and he sought to repay the debts through austerity, with rationing of food, electricity and heating. In 1987 more than 20,000 workers marched in Brașov against these policies. A coup planned for October 1984 failed when the unit assigned to it was sent to harvest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 17 December 1989 Ceaușescu ordered the military to open fire on demonstrators in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timisoara">Timișoara</a>, causing many deaths and injuries. The protests reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bucharest">Bucharest</a>, and he and his wife Elena fled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> but were soon captured after the armed forces turned on them. A show trial convicted both of economic sabotage and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>, and they were executed by firing squad on 25 December, ending 42 years of Communist rule. Opinion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> on his rule remains divided, and polling has found that many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> retain admiration for him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicolae Ceaușescu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Nicolae Ceaușescu</b>'s government later allowed it to be played and sung in public, but it was never the anthem of the Socialist Republic of Romania.</blockquote><a href="/songs/desteapta-te-romane">Deșteaptă-te, române!</a></li><li><blockquote>The revolution of December 1989 overthrew <b>Nicolae Ceaușescu</b> and ended the Socialist Republic of Romania.</blockquote><a href="/state/romania">Romania</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Nicolae Ceaușescu</b>, leader from 1965, renamed the country the Socialist Republic, refused to join the 1968 invasion of Czechoslovakia and won Western favour, but built a personality cult, banned abortion in 1966 and imposed severe austerity in the 1980s to pay off foreign debt.</blockquote><a href="/state/socialist-republic-of-romania">Socialist Republic of Romania</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Scornicești, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. Died in Târgoviște, in what is now <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.6,24.55&amp;year=1964" aria-label="Scornicești on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5136 587 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Nicolae Ceaușescu"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5586 857h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5586 857h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.6,24.55&amp;year=1964">What was here in 1964? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
